event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1955,"item_id":"fc_088631ca57ad9a65016adb1d773f6081918956093ac3f8135f","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"spec\\\":{\\\"format_version\\\":2,\\\"case_id\\\":\\\"wave-map-energy\\\",\\\"full_target\\\":\\\"Let n,m be positive integers. Let u:R^n×(0,∞)→R^m be jointly smooth, and let q∈R^m have Euclidean norm one. Assume (i) |u(x,r)|=1 for all x,r>0; (ii) for each x,r>0 there exists a∈R with u_tt(x,r)-Δ_xu(x,r)=a u(x,r); (iii) on every compact time interval J⊂(0,∞), there is compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q for x∉C_J and r∈J. Define e(x,r)=|u_t(x,r)|²+Σ_{j=1}^n|∂_{x_j}u(x,r)|² and E(r)=∫_{R^n}e(x,r)dx with Lebesgue measure and Euclidean norms. Prove e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable for every r>0, E is differentiable at every r>0, and E'(r)=0.\\\",\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"For every r>0, e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable; E is differentiable at r and E'(r)=0.\\\",\\\"parameters\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"n\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"positive integer\\\"},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"m\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"positive integer\\\"},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"u\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"jointly smooth map R^n×(0,∞)→R^m\\\"},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"q\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"vector in R^m with Euclidean norm one\\\"}],\\\"hypotheses\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H001\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every x∈R^n and r>0, |u(x,r)|=1, where |·| is the Euclidean norm.\\\",\\\"origin\\\":\\\"frozen-target.md lines 7-10\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H002\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every x∈R^n and r>0 there exists a∈R such that u_tt(x,r)-Δ_xu(x,r)=a u(x,r), with Δ_x the componentwise spatial Laplacian.\\\",\\\"origin\\\":\\\"frozen-target.md lines 11-13\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H003\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every compact interval J⊂(0,∞) there is a compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C_J and r∈J.\\\",\\\"origin\\\":\\\"frozen-target.md lines 14-16\\\"}],\\\"definitions\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"D001\\\",\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"e\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"R^n×(0,∞)→R\\\",\\\"definition\\\":\\\"e(x,r)=|u_t(x,r)|²+Σ_{j=1}^n|∂_{x_j}u(x,r)|²\\\",\\\"depends_on\\\":[],\\\"requires\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"x\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"R^n\\\"},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"r\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"(0,∞)\\\"}],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"n\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"D002\\\",\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"E\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"(0,∞)→R\\\",\\\"definition\\\":\\\"E(r)=∫_{R^n}e(x,r) dx, Lebesgue integral\\\",\\\"depends_on\\\":[\\\"D001\\\"],\\\"requires\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"r\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"(0,∞)\\\"}],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"e\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"D003\\\",\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"Δ_x\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"spatial componentwise Laplacian on R^m-valued maps\\\",\\\"definition\\\":\\\"Δ_xu=Σ_{j=1}^n∂²_{x_j}u, component by component\\\",\\\"depends_on\\\":[],\\\"requires\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"n\\\"]}],\\\"logic\\\":{\\\"classical\\\":true,\\\"rules\\\":[\\\"direct\\\",\\\"modus_ponens\\\",\\\"forall_introduction\\\",\\\"implication_introduction\\\",\\\"contradiction_classical\\\"],\\\"calculations\\\":[\\\"Finite sums and Euclidean inner-product differentiation; one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus; uniform convergence on compact boxes; Fubini for continuous compactly supported functions.\\\"],\\\"primitives\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"R\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"real numbers\\\",\\\"meaning\\\":\\\"the real field with its usual topology, order, and Lebesgue measure\\\"},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"R^k\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"Euclidean space\\\",\\\"meaning\\\":\\\"k-dimensional real Euclidean space with Lebesgue measure and Euclidean inner product\\\"}]},\\\"pins\\\":{\\\"lean_version\\\":\\\"4.32.2\\\",\\\"mathlib_commit\\\":\\\"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\\\",\\\"mathlib_root\\\":\\\"/Users/syyui/formalization-tools/lean-mathlib-v4.32.2/mathlib\\\"},\\\"inputs\\\":[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"frozen-target.md\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"problem.md\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"proof-draft.json\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"source-context.md\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"}]},\\\"graph\\\":{\\\"format_version\\\":2,\\\"case_id\\\":\\\"wave-map-energy\\\",\\\"spec_sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\",\\\"propositions\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H001\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every x∈R^n and r>0, |u(x,r)|=1, where |·| is the Euclidean norm.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H002\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every x∈R^n and r>0 there exists a∈R such that u_tt(x,r)-Δ_xu(x,r)=a u(x,r), with Δ_x the componentwise spatial Laplacian.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H003\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every compact interval J⊂(0,∞) there is a compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C_J and r∈J.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"q\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"target\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every r>0, e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable; E is differentiable at r and E'(r)=0.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\"]}],\\\"inferences\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R001\\\",\\\"inputs\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"output\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"rules\\\":[\\\"direct\\\",\\\"forall_introduction\\\"],\\\"locals\\\":[],\\\"argument\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Fix r0>0 and choose a compact interval J⊂(0,∞) having r0 in its interior. By H003 choose compact C_J. For r∈J, u=q on the open complement of C_J, so all first derivatives vanish there; continuity makes them vanish on the boundary too. Thus e(·,r) is continuous, bounded on C_J and zero outside C_J, hence Lebesgue integrable. Choose a closed box B containing C_J. For r near r0, E(r)=∫_B e(x,r)dx. Smoothness gives continuous e_t on B×J, uniformly continuous there. The fundamental theorem of calculus expresses the difference quotient of e at r0 as the θ∈[0,1] average of e_t(x,r0+θh); uniform continuity gives uniform convergence in x∈B to e_t(x,r0). Since B has finite measure, integrating the difference quotients proves E'(r0)=∫_B e_t(x,r0)dx. Differentiate the finite sum defining e: e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2Σ_j u_j·∂_j u_t. For each j, u_t·u_j is smooth and compactly supported; integrating its x_j derivative over R^n gives zero by the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus and Fubini, since that derivative is continuous and compactly supported. Therefore ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_j=−∫u_t·u_jj and E'(r0)=2∫u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu). Differentiating |u|²=1 in time yields u·u_t=0. By H002, u_tt−Δ_xu=a u pointwise for some scalar a, so u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=0. Hence E'(r0)=0. Since r0 was arbitrary, the target follows.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\",\\\"J\\\",\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"B\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\",\\\"u_t\\\",\\\"u_j\\\",\\\"u_{tt}\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]}],\\\"applications\\\":[]} ]}}\"}"}

