.md\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"problem.md\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"proof-draft.json\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"source-context.md\\\",\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\"}]},\\\"graph\\\":{\\\"format_version\\\":2,\\\"case_id\\\":\\\"wave-map-energy\\\",\\\"spec_sha256\\\":\\\"placeholder\\\",\\\"propositions\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H001\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every x∈R^n and r>0, |u(x,r)|=1, where |·| is the Euclidean norm.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H002\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every x∈R^n and r>0 there exists a∈R such that u_tt(x,r)-Δ_xu(x,r)=a u(x,r), with Δ_x the componentwise spatial Laplacian.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H003\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every compact interval J⊂(0,∞) there is a compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C_J and r∈J.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"q\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"target\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every r>0, e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable; E is differentiable at r and E'(r)=0.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\"]}],\\\"inferences\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R001\\\",\\\"inputs\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"output\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"rules\\\":[\\\"direct\\\",\\\"forall_introduction\\\"],\\\"locals\\\":[],\\\"argument\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Fix r0>0 and choose a compact interval J⊂(0,∞) having r0 in its interior. By H003 choose compact C_J. For r∈J, u=q on the open complement of C_J, so all first derivatives vanish there; continuity makes them vanish on the boundary too. Thus e(·,r) is continuous, bounded on C_J and zero outside C_J, hence Lebesgue integrable. Choose a closed box B containing C_J. For r near r0, E(r)=∫_B e(x,r)dx. Smoothness gives continuous e_t on B×J, uniformly continuous there. The fundamental theorem of calculus expresses the difference quotient of e at r0 as the θ∈[0,1] average of e_t(x,r0+θh); uniform continuity gives uniform convergence in x∈B to e_t(x,r0). Since B has finite measure, integrating the difference quotients proves E'(r0)=∫_B e_t(x,r0)dx. Differentiate the finite sum defining e: e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2Σ_j u_j·∂_j u_t. For each j, u_t·u_j is smooth and compactly supported; integrating its x_j derivative over R^n gives zero by the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus and Fubini, since that derivative is continuous and compactly supported. Therefore ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_j=−∫u_t·u_jj and E'(r0)=2∫u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu). Differentiating |u|²=1 in time yields u·u_t=0. By H002, u_tt−Δ_xu=a u pointwise for some scalar a, so u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=0. Hence E'(r0)=0. Since r0 was arbitrary, the target follows.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\",\\\"J\\\",\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"B\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\",\\\"u_t\\\",\\\"u_j\\\",\\\"u_{tt}\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]}],\\\"applications\\\":[]} ]}}\"}","status":"completed"}}

